<SiteLayout>
  <PageHeader
    title={"Solving Politics with Prediction Markets"}
    lastUpdated={lastUpdated}
  />
  <p>
    Can you believe that, in the year 2024, there are still trivial epistemic
    differences that make up some large amount of political discourse? People
    don't just disagree with each other; they disagree with each other because
    they're ignoring or unaware of things that are common knowledge, giving no
    epistemic baseline from which to begin.
  </p>
  <p>
    I think that we can resolve the boring differences by creating prediction
    markets about every policy. There will still be disagreements - prediction
    markets are forecasts, not the actual ground truth that is yet unknown, and
    there are still values-differences that remain here on Ancient Earth where{
      " "
    }
    <a
      href="https://bsky.app/profile/sophaelia.thetetra.space/post/3kz3xtodbes2g"
      >everyone is bounded</a
    > and maybe beyond, but these will be interesting disagreements, focusing on places
    where people <em>actually</em> disagree and aren't just failing to incorporate
    available evidence.
  </p>
  <p>
    So, my mission, if I choose to accept it: <strong
      >full branch coverage</strong
    > of politics. Then people will be able to vote on their values and bet on their
    beliefs. Like they should have been doing, the whole time.
  </p>
  <p>
    Markets on elections that happened in the past will be underneath
    collapsibles and will probably not be up to date. Markets on elections that
    are in the future will not be underneath collapsibles.
  </p>
  <p>
    A disclaimer: I will have prediction markets, and I will have commentary
    about those markets. The commentary is accurate to the prediction markets as
    of the time of writing.
  </p>
  <h2>The Next UK General Election</h2>
  <p>
    The next UK general election is currently scheduled for on or before 15th
    August 2029, though it's very plausible that it might be called early.
  </p>
  <Manifold slug="Noit/when-will-the-next-uk-general-elect-dczh8Uc6QE" />
  <p>
    As of writing, prediction markets give a 45% chance it's held early, before
    the start of 2029; with a mean date of July 2028.
  </p>
  <Manifold slug="Tetraspace/when-will-the-next-uk-general-elect-uccgggpqCE" />
  <p>
    By year, there's a 29% chance it's held in 2028, a 55% chance it's held in
    2029, and a small probability scattered over years earlier than 2028. So
    prior to the end of 2028, we can mostly assume that Labour is going to be in
    power, barring something surprising happening, though perhaps with a new
    leader.
  </p>
  <Manifold slug="Tetraspace/which-party-will-the-prime-minister-usspd5PZOC" />
  <p>
    After the election, it looks like it's going to be one of three parties
    either getting a majority in Parliament or making up a lead role in a
    coalition: <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Labour_Party_(UK)"
      >Labour</a
    > (34%), <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Reform_UK">Reform</a> (35%), or
    <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Conservative_Party_(UK)"
      >Conservatives</a
    > (14%), with special mention to <a
      href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Green_Party_of_England_and_Wales"
      >Green</a
    > (8%).
  </p>

  <img
    src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/6e/Opinion_polling_graph_for_the_next_United_Kingdom_general_election_%28post-2024%29.svg"
    alt='A graph of polling data on the UK election, found on the "Opinion polling for the next United Kingdom general election" article on Wikipedia.'
    class="inline-image"
  />

  <p>
    This represents a realignment between the major parties following the 2024
    election, visible very clearly in the polls, and indicated in the <a
      href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025_United_Kingdom_local_elections"
      >2025 local elections</a
    >; Reform's polling has risen to position it above all the previous major
    parties at 25%, and Green's polling has risen to position it on par with
    Labour and the Conservatives at around 17%. Given current polling, Reform is
    on track to replace the existing right-wing party, and the Green party is a
    contender to replace the existing left-wing party. A time of uncertainty,
    for those without prediction markets! (Those with them are still uncertain,
    but less, and quantifiably.)
  </p>

  <h3>Policy after the Next Election</h3>
  <p>
    My conditional policy markets will be of the form "if the Prime Minister is
    of party X in the year Y, what will metric Z be"? This captures the effect
    of elections - a different party winning an elections indeed does change the
    party of the prime minister - and also takes into account uncertainty in
    election timing.
  </p>

